Measured, and it reverses my earlier reasoning — the task file was right about `run_git_output`, for a reason I had not identified. Path compares by components, so:

• `run_git_in_dir(Path::new("."), &["revert",…])` → guard resolves target relative to `dir` itself = the *relative* `.`, which never equals the absolute manifest dir. Guard silently stops firing.
• `run_git_output(&["-C", ".", "revert",…]) → guard resolves -C . against the *absolute process cwd* = /repo/.`, which does equal manifest dir. Guard still fires.

So run_git_in_dir would have quietly weakened a safety guard. run_git_output with an explicit -C is both the local idiom (the sibling amend seams) and the safe one. Writing the seam now.
